adding-forces-answer-why = The load hangs still — so the forces on it balance: its weight with the ruler pulls it down, the dynamometers pull it up. So the resultant of the dynamometers’ forces equals the load’s weight each time — the very force one dynamometer showed. Hold it by one dynamometer or by two, share the weight between the ruler’s ends equally or not (the end nearer the weights holds more — why, we shall see with the lever), — together upwards it is the same force every time. And when the second dynamometer pulls the weights down, the upper one has to hold both the load and that pull — it shows more. The resultant of the two opposite forces — their difference — again equals the load’s weight.
adding-forces-answer-say = Each time R = { $sums } — as much as one dynamometer showed.
adding-forces-text-parts = One dynamometer held the load with { $one }
adding-forces-text-parts-2 = two, the weights at { $at } cm: { $f } + { $f2 } = { $f3 }
adding-forces-text-parts-3 = up { $down }, down { $down2 }: { $down } − { $down2 } = { $down3 }
